urity  there  yes  first when we saw  the crisis measures from the european  commission that there is a new emergency  emergency support program  with an amount of 330 million  euros and  the commission is very active  to  get  to get  the products  to the ukrainian to the ukrainian people  but that's not um that's not that easy  because normally we should uh have the  possibility to establish a so-called  green corridor to guarantee an export  from ukraine but  imports as well from the european union  and this is still a challenge to to  establish or  to introduce this  green  this green corridor  and can we expect rising consumer food  prices due to the ukraine crisis  and what can the eu do to tackle this  yeah so we see that all the prices for  food are raising since months so this  was a  trend before the ukraine war but it's  clear that now  we the the the  raise of prices  remain still or will be higher  than before  we see that with meat we see that with  corn  and this has different  backgrounds for example the price for  the ingredients for  farmers are higher than before  especially the price for fertilizers  they we can  say more or less they are four times  higher than one year before so there you  see the production costs for the farmer  are much more higher than one year ago  yeah and in this sense  how is the european union helping these  farmers  yeah first  we use  the first time in this cup period the  crisis reserve and i appreciate that the  crisis  reserve has an about amount of 500  million euros and this can be tripled by  the member states for example my own  member state germany  offers now an amount of 180 million  euros  to the farmers that they can deal  with the higher production costs  and  and so on so this is one  one instrument and the commission opened  the possibility for member states to  support to support farmers when it comes  to fertilizers not that they can benefit  from a  state aid  from the member states to buy  fertilize